## Code Snippet

1. Responsive Layout Fix (Mobile Optimization)

@media (max-width: 768px){
  .recipe-content{
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .ingredients,
  .directions{
    width: 100%;
  }
}

Explanation:
While building my recipe detail pages, I noticed that the ingredients and 
directions sections were overflowing on smaller screens. This was caused by using
a horizontal flex layout with fixed widths.
To solve this, I changed the layout to a vertical column on mobile devices and 
allowed both sections to take full width. This prevented horizontal scrolling and
improved readability.

2. CSS Variables for Design Consistency

:root{
  --c-primary:#DC143C;
  --c-bg:#ffffff;
  --c-text:#222;
  --c-muted:#666;
}

Explanation:
In class, we learned how to use CSS variables, but I expanded this concept into 
a more consistent design system. Instead of repeating color values throughout my 
CSS, I defined reusable variables and applied them across multiple components.
This made the code more maintainable and ensured visual consistency across 
different pages.

3. Responsive Navigation Adjustment

@media (max-width: 768px){
  .category-nav {
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 6px;
  }

  .category-nav a {
    flex: 1;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 11px;
  }
}

Explanation:
I adjusted the navigation layout for mobile screens so that all category buttons 
stay on a single row. By using flex: 1, each button automatically takes equal 
width, and reducing the font size prevents overflow.
This improves usability on smaller devices and keeps the interface clean and
organized.

Reflection

These snippets show how I applied concepts from class—such as media queries and 
CSS variables—to solve real design problems.
Instead of only using basic layouts, I adapted my design for different screen 
sizes and improved the overall user experience. In particular, I focused on 
preventing layout overflow and maintaining consistency across components.


---
## Media Query

I used mobile as my media Query

@media (max-width: 768px)

On mobile:
*Layout switches to single column
*Navigation becomes compact and stays on one row 
*Font sizes and spacing are reduced for usability


